NumPy for EDA Acceleration

1. vectorized operations:
    Instead of looping through elements (as in pure Python),
        NumPy applies operations to entire arrays at once
    So, codes are shorter, faster, and more readable
    You can quickly transform entire columns of a DataFrame
        Pandas columns are built on NumPy arrays
    
2. Broadcasting:
    NumPy automatically stretches arrays of different shapes to make opertions possible
    eg:
    arr = np.array([1, 2, 3]) # shape (3,)
    matrix = np.array([
        [10],
        [20],
        [30]
    ])  # shape(3, 1)
    print(arr + matrix) # shape (3, 3)

    Broadcasting Rules:
        1. Compare shapes element-wise from right to left
        2. If dimensions differ:
            a. If one is 1, it can be stretched
            b. If they don't match and neither is 1, error

3. Random Numbers in NumPy
    Why random numbers matters?
        a. Simulations
        b. Sampling
        c. Synthetic datasets
        d. Noise injection

    NumPy's random module
        a. Uniform distribution
            Every number in range has equal probability
        b. Normal (Gaussian) distribution
            Bell-shaped curve defined by mean and SD 
        c. Integers
            Random integers within a range
        d. Choice/Permutation
            Randomly select elements from an array
            Shuffle arrays or sample without replacement

        Seed:
            Random numbers are generated using a pseudo-random algorithm
            Setting a seed ensures reproducibility
                i.e same random numbers every time you run the code 
        
        Shape:
            You can generate arrays of random numbers directly by specifying shape of array 
        